[Music]  foreign  this is your Tech news briefing for  Thursday November 3rd I'm Julie Chang  for the Wall Street Journal filling in  for Zoe Thomas  as concerns about hacking or other cyber  crime continue to grow Europe has put in  place new cyber security rules for its  aviation industry and it's not just  airlines that are being tasked with  complying with them but all those up and  down the supply chain from plane  manufacturers to weather data providers  and even Caterers here to tell us more  about these new requirements and how  companies are responding to them is wsja  pro cyber security reporter Catherine  stopp Catherine thank you for joining us  thank you for having me so Catherine  tell us some more about these new  Aviation cyber security rules what  exactly are they who would be affected  by them  so these are new cyber security rules  applying in the European Union from the  European Aviation regulator they apply  to the aviation sector and what's kind  of unique about them they're pretty  Broad in that they don't just apply to  Big Airlines and airports they apply  really across the supply chain to a lot  of smaller companies so that includes  like flight training schools aerodromes  but also these small companies that  provide services related to things like  Air Cargo or flight data  and they basically require companies to  start using software to track and  identify cyber threats that could pose a  risk to flight safety and then to  implement some kind of security measures  that actually prevent and protect  against those threats and that comes  with other kinds of governance  requirements so that companies really  need to appoint somebody internally who  will oversee their compliance with these  regulations who interact with the  regulators and things like that and do  we know why these rules were put in  place  so actually these rules have been in the  works for a number of years it's not  really because of a recent uptick or  anything that happened this year related  to cyber attacks but the people I spoke  to including at the European Aviation  regulator said that what they wanted to  prevent here is any kind of major Cyber  attack  so you know we hear cyber attacks we  hear flights obviously it worries people  How concerned should the flying public  be about potential hacking in the  aviation industry I mean is there a  danger to safety  there haven't been major cyber attacks  affecting flight safety so far  it is a nightmare scenario that people  think about and when we read headlines  or hear about cyber attacks affecting an  airline for instance that always gets  people's attention it gets people afraid  often these cyber attacks are  superficial or really low-level attacks  that affect something like a website so  last month there were cyber attacks on a  number of U.S airports websites that  actually took them down temporarily and  that was in the news about these cyber  attacks affecting airports but really  those attacks didn't have any effects  Beyond just taking down the websites in  some cases for a number of hours it was  however something that I think got a lot  of people's attention  you said that these new rules have been  in the works for a while but were there  other cyber security rules that had  already existed in this space  yes so in Europe there are cyber  security requirements for critical  infrastructure providers and that  includes a lot of airlines airports and  the requirements include reporting cyber  attacks to National regulators and  implementing some basic cyber security  protections but these new rules affect a  broader range of companies in the supply  chain that previously didn't fall under  that category of critical infrastructure  providers so that's in Europe and in the  US the Transport Security Administration  recently said they're going to introduce  some new cyber security rules for the  aviation sector and they already do have  some for airports and Airlines  and when do the new rules go into effect  they'll have to start complying by 2025.  okay so they have some time you've been  speaking with people in the aviation  industry what has their response been so  far this has definitely gotten people's  attention in the aviation industry and  they're all saying we need to start  working on complying with this really  soon not just wait until 2025 because it  could be a lot of work for us it isn't  something that's getting a huge amount  of attention outside of the aviation  industry it maybe is seen as more of a  niche technical thing but people who  work on cyber security in aviation are  definitely working on this and well  aware of what they have to do  half of the companies mentioned any  potential challenges they might come  across one of the challenges is  companies will need to set up a whole  cyber risk management system that could  mean you know buying new software  training staff on how to use it and  hiring people to actually oversee their  program make sure they're doing things  right companies in lots of different  sectors so not just Aviation are really  struggling to find cyber security staff  right now there's just a huge labor Gap  in cyber security and the people I  talked to said especially in something  really specialized like Aviation finding  people who understand  cyber security in a technical area like  Aviation could be especially difficult  so  for bigger companies this could be you  know business as usual they have maybe  big cyber security teams but for smaller  companies in particular it could be  really hard to find the right people and  also to offer them salaries to lure them  away from wherever they work currently  and with the shortage of cyber security  workers and Aviation being a very  specialized field as you mentioned do we  have a sense for how much all of this  will cost and if those costs would  trickle down to customers or Airlines  bottom lines the cost likely won't be  very significant for bigger companies  but small ones will have to think about  whether they need to hire new staff do  staff training buy software to help them  manage cyber risks this new law it  doesn't require companies go out and buy  really fancy specific new equipment  that's going to be very expensive so  it's likely not going to be  huge but especially for some of the  small companies this could be  significant for them  all right that is wsj pro cyber security  reporter Catherine stepp Catherine thank  you again for being here thank you and  that is it for today's Tech news  briefing as always you can find more  Tech stories on our website wsj.com I'm  Julie Chang for the Wall Street Journal  thank you for listening  foreign  [Music]  [Music]

